\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Dressed transport and charged two-point functions}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For \vocabk{massive graded Wilson data}{fdip-00IN}, write #{i_z:S_z\to\mathcal V} for the inclusion of a site spin fiber, #{p_z=i_z^{\dagger}}, #{\psi_z=p_z\psi} and #{\bar\psi_z=\bar\psi i_z}. Let #{\gamma:y\to x} be any labeled oriented path, using inverse links for reversed traversal. Its charge-#{q} transport #{h_\gamma:S_y\to S_x} obeys #{h_\gamma^g=g_x^qh_\gamma g_y^{-q}}. For #{M\in\operatorname{End}(S_x)} use the canonical spin-fiber identifications and put
##{B_{\gamma,M}=\bar\psi_x(Mh_\gamma\psi_y).}
This dressed bilocal is gauge invariant, and the \vocabk{coupled coefficient functional}{fdip-00IT} satisfies
##{\langle B_{\gamma,M}\rangle_U=-\trace_{S_x}\left(Mh_\gamma p_yK_U^{-1}i_x\right).}
The alternative contraction #{\trace_{S_y}(h_\gamma^{-1}p_xK_U^{-1}i_y)} is also gauge invariant. For distinct vertices #{x\ne y},
##{\int p_xK_U^{-1}i_y\,\extd\nu(U)=0.}
On every allowed lattice the empty path at #{x} with #{M=\bbI_S} satisfies
##{\operatorname{Re}\langle\bar\psi_x(\psi_x)\rangle_U
\le-\frac{n_sm}{M_0^2}<0,\qquad M_0=m+d/a+4dc_W/a^2.}
The same bound holds after the connection average.}

\proof{
\p{Gauge covariance gives #{p_xK_{U^g}^{-1}i_y=g_x^q(p_xK_U^{-1}i_y)g_y^{-q}}. The phases cancel in the bilocal. Contract the endomorphism-valued insertion identity in \ref{fdip-00IV} with #{Mh_\gamma} and reverse the two odd factors. This gives the trace and its minus sign. The two maps inside that trace have opposite endpoints: #{h_\gamma:S_y\to S_x} and #{p_yK_U^{-1}i_x:S_x\to S_y}.}

\p{Invariance of #{\nu} forces the averaged map #{p_xK_U^{-1}i_y} to equal #{g_x^qg_y^{-q}} times itself. Since #{q\ne0} and #{x,y} are distinct vertices, this multiplier can be chosen different from one, so the map is zero. Distinct integer representatives of a single torus vertex do not satisfy this hypothesis.}

\p{For the last bound, write #{v=K_Uw}. Accretivity and #{\|K_U\|\le M_0} give
##{\operatorname{Re}\langle v,K_U^{-1}v\rangle
=\operatorname{Re}\langle K_Uw,w\rangle\ge m\|w\|^2\ge\frac m{M_0^2}\|v\|^2.}
Taking the trace of this inequality on the full spin fiber, and using the negative bilocal sign, proves the result. It is uniform in #{U} and therefore survives averaging, including on a one-vertex lattice.}
}

\texfig{\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=huge]
S_y \arrow[r,bend left=28,"h_\gamma"] &
S_x \arrow[l,bend left=28,"p_yK_U^{-1}i_x"]
\end{tikzcd}}

\p{The opposite arrows compose to an endomorphism of #{S_x}; its trace after #{M} enters the bilocal expectation with a minus sign.}
